<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <!--
        Make the Odoo accounting groups a hierarchy (#474).

        MEASURED on a live tenant: a user in "Accounting / Administrator"
        (account.group_account_manager) had

            has_group('account.group_account_manager') -> True
            has_group('account.group_account_user')    -> False
            has_group('account.group_account_readonly')-> False

        In Odoo 19 these three groups are declared independently. Every ACL in
        ncollection_account_reports is keyed on group_account_readonly ("Show
        Accounting Features - Readonly"), so the entire native report suite —
        Trial Balance, General Ledger, Balance Sheet, P&L, the VAT 201, all of
        it — raised AccessError for the MOST privileged accounting role, and
        Odoo's menu filter then hid every one of those menus. The reports were
        installed, licensed and unreachable.

        This restores the conventional containment readonly < user < manager.
        It widens nothing that matters: `readonly` is a feature-VISIBILITY
        group, and a user who already holds `manager` has full access to
        account.move. ACLs and record rules are untouched and still apply — a
        reader still cannot write a report run, and the ir.rule from #413 still
        scopes each run to its creator.

        Done here rather than by rewriting 24 ACL rows: one statement of the
        group relationship, in the base module every ncollection_account_*
        depends on, instead of the same decision repeated per model.
    -->
    <record id="account.group_account_user" model="res.groups">
        <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('account.group_account_readonly'))]"/>
    </record>

    <record id="account.group_account_manager" model="res.groups">
        <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('account.group_account_user'))]"/>
    </record>
</odoo>
